    <h2 id="effect" style="margin-top:0">1. Physics that works for every thrower</h2>
    <p>This is not a baseball thing. It is physics. Any object you throw (a baseball, a softball, a football) obeys the same rules. Whatever energy your body builds has to transfer through the last point of contact: your fingertips. Whatever leaks into the palm never makes it into the ball.</p>
    <p>That single moment decides the three pillars of throwing better, and SpinGrip helps all three:</p>
    <div class="pillars"><div><b>Velocity</b><span>How hard the ball comes out.</span></div><div><b>Spin</b><span>How much the ball rides, carries, breaks or spirals.</span></div><div><b>Accuracy</b><span>Whether it goes exactly where you aimed it.</span></div></div>
    <p>Here is the part most players miss: all three are decided in the same split second, the release. Your legs and torso build the energy. Your fingertips decide how much of it actually gets into the ball, and in what direction. Finish out front on the fingertips and all three move at once.</p>
    <p>The SpinGrip Effect is that this happens automatically. The glove creates space between the palm and the ball, so the correct physics is the path of least resistance. You do not have to think about it, cue it or film it. Every rep teaches the same clean release, whether you are a pitcher, an infielder, a softball player or a quarterback.</p>
    <p>Extension ties into this too. Extension is how far out front you let the ball go. The further out front you release, the shorter the ball’s trip to the target: a 90 mph fastball with big extension plays like 92, a throw from the outfield gets there flatter and sooner, and a pass drops into a tighter window.</p>
    <figure><img src="{{ 'kg-space-between-palm-ball.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Elite pitchers at release, each with space between the palm and the ball" width="1600" height="1275" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Space between the palm and the ball at release: the common thread among elite arms.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2 id="release">2. The release: where all three are won</h2>
    <p>Everything above builds energy. The release decides whether you keep it. When the ball sinks into the palm, slips early, or rolls off the side of the hand, you lose speed, you lose spin, and the ball misses your spot. Worse, it happens in milliseconds, far too fast to feel or fix by thinking about it mid-throw.</p>
    <p>A great release is simple to describe and hard to repeat: space between the palm and the ball, pressure staying on the fingertips, and the ball let go out front. That is the release that produces high spin, clean velocity, repeatable accuracy and real extension.</p>
    <p>That is exactly the problem SpinGrip was built to solve. It is the first throwing glove that creates that space between the palm and the ball and feeds immediate feedback into the athlete’s feel, on the throw itself, at real speed. The right release stops being an accident you get once a week and becomes the one your body owns and repeats.</p>
