    <h2 id="pitchers">3. For pitchers: spin, velo and pinpoint command</h2>
    <p>Pitchers are not just trying to throw hard. You want a fastball that rides, a breaking ball that bites, a changeup that dies, and all of it landing where you called it. Every one of those is a release story.</p>
    <ul><li><strong>Spin rate and spin efficiency</strong> come from clean fingertip pressure at the last instant. Choke the ball in the palm and you bleed both.</li><li><strong>Movement</strong> on every pitch is spin applied in a consistent direction. Consistent release point, consistent shape.</li><li><strong>Command</strong> is repeatability. Same finish, same slot, same spot.</li><li><strong>Extension</strong> makes your stuff play harder without adding a single mph.</li></ul>
    <p><strong>How to use SpinGrip:</strong> wear it from your very first warm-up throw and work all the way up through bullpen, long toss and flat grounds with it on. When you are warm, pull it off and let it fly; most arms describe a fifth gear, because the feel stays with you. Pitchers should clear in-game use with their umpire or league first.</p>
    <h2 id="position">4. For position players: stronger, truer throws</h2>
    <p>Infielders, outfielders and catchers are graded on a different set of results, but they come from the same release.</p>
    <ul><li><strong>A stronger throw</strong> across the diamond or from the gap, without max effort.</li><li><strong>Better carry.</strong> Backspin created at the fingertips makes a ball hold its line instead of dying halfway.</li><li><strong>Truer ballflight.</strong> No tailing, cutting or sinking throws that pull your teammate off the bag.</li><li><strong>Accuracy under rush.</strong> A release you own holds up when you are off balance and hurrying.</li><li><strong>Extension</strong> gets the ball out front and on a line, which shows up as pop times and outs.</li></ul>
    <p><strong>How to use SpinGrip:</strong> wear it for pregame catch, defensive work, drill work and long toss. Position players can also train and play in it.</p>
    <h2 id="qbs">5. For quarterbacks: stronger throws, tighter spirals</h2>
    <p>A football is a different shape, but the physics are identical. Velocity, spin and accuracy are still the three pillars, and they are still decided at the fingertips out front. With a football you can see the release with your own eyes: it is the spiral.</p>
    <ul><li><strong>Stronger throws.</strong> When the ball sits off the palm and comes off the fingertips, energy goes into the ball instead of dying in your hand.</li><li><strong>Tighter spirals.</strong> A clean index-finger finish means a tight, nose-down ball that cuts wind and rain instead of wobbling.</li><li><strong>Precise accuracy.</strong> A repeatable release point puts the ball on the receiver’s frame.</li><li><strong>Extension.</strong> Letting it go out front flattens the throw so you can fit it into tighter windows.</li></ul>
    <p>Most QBs describe SpinGrip Football as the ball suddenly feeling light, like a Nerf ball, because they are finally getting it off the fingertips every time. Wear it for warm-ups, route trees, footwork drills and 7-on-7 work.</p>
