    <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin:0;max-width:none"><details class="acc"><summary>How much velocity can I realistically add?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Most healthy throwers who stick to a real throwing and strength program add 2–5 mph over an off-season. Bigger jumps usually come from fixing one mechanical leak, cleaning up the release, or natural growth in younger athletes.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Why do spin and accuracy matter as much as velocity?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Velocity gets the ball there. Spin makes it move or carry, and accuracy decides whether it plays. All three come from the same place: how cleanly the ball leaves your fingertips.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>What is extension and why should I care?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Extension is how far out front you release the ball. More extension means the ball is on the hitter sooner and the pitch plays harder than the radar reading. For position players, releasing out front means a flatter, truer throw.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Does long toss actually build velocity?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Yes. Long toss builds arm strength, mobility and intent. It works best when you pull down on a line back to normal distance so the effort carries over to the mound or the field.</p></div></details><details class="acc"><summary>Where does grip fit in?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="acc__body"><p>A late, clean fingertip release keeps energy in the ball instead of leaking it into the palm. SpinGrip is the first throwing glove that gives you that feel instantly, at real training speed, so the good release becomes the one you repeat.</p></div></details></div>
